WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.920
Is The Nightmare Before Christmas a Halloween
movie or a Christmas movie?

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.360
Sure, viewers of all ages can indulge in the
stylish animated film for months on end and

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.820
still be in season, but no matter when you
watch, there are probably some things you

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.240
notice in the holiday classic as an adult.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.820
It's no secret that Nightmare Before Christmas
writer and producer Tim Burton is a huge fan

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.460
of classic monster films.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.360
Early in his career, he directed the animated
short Vincent, in which a young boy dreams

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.960
of becoming like his idol, the beloved classic
horror master Vincent Price.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.260
"He doesn't mind living with his sister, dog,
and cats.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.400
Though he'd rather share a home with spiders,
roots, and bats."

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:47.399
Shortly after, Burton directed the short Frankenweenie,
which would be expanded to a full-length animated

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.880
feature in 2012.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.760
In both versions, a boy named Victor Frankenstein
resurrects his beloved pet dog.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.339
This love of monsters of movies past comes
through loud and clear in Halloween Town,

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.720
where The Nightmare Before Christmas takes
place.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.500
The entire populace is monstrous in appearance,
and adults will notice that they're frequently

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.789
references to specific literary and film monsters.

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:13.950
Dr. Finkelstein, the mad scientist who created
Sally and helps Jack conjure up a skeleton

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.980
sled team, very obviously draws on Burton's
love Dr. Victor Frankenstein.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:25.760
Just about every monster in town is a reference
to a classic: Igor, the Wolfman, Mr. Hyde,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.040
the Undersea Gal, Mummy Boy, the Vampires.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.060
You name a classic, and you'll find them living
in Halloween Town.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.920
There aren't a whole lot of kids movies about
having a midlife crisis, but The Nightmare

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.120
Before Christmas is an exception.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.900
Jack's been the Pumpkin King for far too long
and sees himself stuck in a rut.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:48.490
He feels bored with his life, so he haphazardly
throws himself into a new obsession.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:53.310
He assumes responsibilities that no one has
asked him to take on and makes a mess that

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.580
basically spans the entire world.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.280
For the most part, kids aren't at risk of
reaching a point where they feel completely

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.080
stagnant in their lives.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.380
The theme of facing an existential void and
dealing with it in destructive ways probably

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.150
isn't too accessible to kids, though they
can probably appreciate that Jack is seeking

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.600
adventure in some way.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.200
For adults, it's far easier to notice the
real-life emotions at the heart of Jack's

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.620
misguided actions.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.140
Throughout The Nightmare Before Christmas,
viewers witness a mostly one-sided romance

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.849
in which Sally, in an endless effort to prove
herself to Jack, is continually subjected

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:31.500
to his oblivious nature and one-track mind.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.740
Obviously, children recognize a romance budding
here, but it takes an experienced heart to

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:41.340
notice that Sally's giving way more than she
should for a guy who's so wrapped up in his

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.000
own existential problems.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.200
Sally's sweet nature and desire to do good
are admirable traits to have, but for most

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.240
of the movie, they're wasted on a pretty selfish
guy.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.460
Sally struggles with feeling acceptance and
joy in her life in Halloween Town in much

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.849
the same way Jack does, but her solution is
to try to make connections and help people

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:04.060
instead of wandering off and leaving her responsibilities
behind.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.820
"I'm restless!

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.060
I can't help it!"

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.200
"It's a phase, my dear.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.280
It'll pass."

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.500
Because this is a children's movie, Jack and
Sally get together in the end, and it appears

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.360
as though Jack has begun to understand how
his actions have consequences.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.460
We hope.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.380
Until he discovers Thanksgiving town or something.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.060
Granted, Sally's obsession with someone she
barely seems to know leads to her creeping

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:33.540
around tombstones to get a look at him, poisoning
her boss, falling asleep outside his house,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.820
and throwing herself out of windows…but
that's another problem entirely.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.579
One of the most adult-oriented gags in The
Nightmare Before Christmas is the character

00:03:42.579 --> 00:03:47.909
of the Mayor, a squat, two-faced humanoid
who's entirely uncertain of how to proceed

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:51.720
with just about anything in Halloween Town
without assurance from his constituents.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.120
Most kids won't pay close enough attention
to politics to understand what makes the idea

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.560
of an incompetent mayor quite so funny, and
a little bit scary.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.680
The Mayor's smiling public face contrasts
with the horror-stricken one on the other

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.439
side of his head, a conceit that tells the
viewer he's performing for the public when

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:15.150
he really has a host of fears about how the
town is run and his responsibility to keep

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.790
it running in an efficient manner.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.190
When the Mayor approaches Jack's home with
plans for the following year's Halloween celebration

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.360
and Jack doesn't answer, the Mayor loses his
cool.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.980
"Jack, please!

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.700
I'm only an elected official here, I can't
make decisions by myself!"

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.580
Politics and hideous monsters have never gone
together so well.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.260
Except in real life.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:41.800
Much like politics, most child viewers probably
don't have much first-hand experience with

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:42.700
casinos.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:48.240
This is, of course, Oogie Boogie's entire
focus, surrounding himself with slot machines,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.300
roulette wheels, and killer cards.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:55.000
When Oogie rolls the dice and announces "Snake
eyes!" it's probably safe to say that the

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.240
kids watching only really get value out of
the snake that actually emerges from the dice,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.980
and not what that roll means from a gambling
perspective.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:07.900
Nor would they gain much insight when Oogie
bangs on the table to reset the dice to read

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.400
eleven, a much more desirable roll in this
context.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.550
But why a miniature gambling world in the
middle of a place that's focused entirely

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.810
on monsters and Halloween mayhem?

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.500
What's the connection?

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.580
The scenes in Oogie Boogie's lair are some
of the most fun, but it's interesting to notice

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.880
that in this town where the dreams of childhood
Halloween reign supreme, the most feared monster

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.000
lives in a grown-up world with high stakes
that serve only himself.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:39.800
When Jack falls into Christmas Town and leaves
overwhelmed by his excitement in this new

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.840
place, he gets the wild idea that he should
try to make Christmas his own.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.260
Without understanding the first thing about
this strange new land, he attempts to engage

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:54.060
in research, but without ever asking any inhabitants
of Christmas Town for their own input or advice.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:57.380
He just takes an entire holiday, no questions
asked.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:03.400
"This year, Christmas will be ours!"

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:10.020
Of course, this arrogant need to just appropriate
whatever he wants ends up in disaster, with

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.080
Jack being shot at as he attempts to spread
Christmas cheer throughout the land.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.200
It's only then, after he's almost blown apart,
that he finally realizes he's in way over

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.870
his head, and he actually decides to ask Santa,
who he kidnapped earlier, what the heck Christmas

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.080
is even about.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.530
Santa, the person who's actually immersed
in the traditions of Christmas Town, decides

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.360
to share those traditions with Jack.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.240
It's a much more genuine interaction that
Jack's attempts to straight-up steal the holiday,

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:44.340
and it introduces the good practice of seeking
out the advice of those with firsthand knowledge

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.960
on a subject, rather than blundering your
way through something blindly.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.620
Kids might not see that message outright,
but adults will notice, and hopefully the

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.520
nuance sticks with younger viewers as they
grow older.

